// face -- the metrics of whichever face is currently loaded.
//
// The default font used to be a constant: 8x8, so one number was the advance,
// the row count and the column count, and txt_cell() answered all three. It is
// now the IBM VGA 8x16 face (glyphs.gen.id), and a face a game loads through
// psf_load may be 8x14 or 12x24, so those three numbers have come apart. They
// live in txt_st, declared beside the table by txt_glyph_data so that the
// metrics and the rows can never disagree:
//
//     txt_st[0]  cell width  -- the advance, and the columns a glyph draws
//     txt_st[1]  cell height -- the rows a glyph draws
//     txt_st[2]  bits per row in the table (the cell width rounded up to a byte)
//     txt_st[3]  how many glyphs, from txt_first() upward
//
// Slot 2 is the one that is not obvious. A bitmap font pads each row out to a
// whole byte, so a 12-wide glyph occupies 16 bits with four of them empty on the
// *right*, and the leftmost column is therefore bit 15 rather than bit 11.
// txt_px_sc shifts against this rather than against the width, which is why a
// non-multiple-of-8 face draws at all.
//
// These read an exported global, so txt_init has to have run. Everything that
// draws text already depended on that -- txt_glyphs had the same requirement --
// and idem_boot's init chain calls it before anything else.

// The rows a glyph occupies in the table, and the pixels it occupies on screen.
txt_cellh() {
} return int (import txt_st)[1];

// The width of one packed row in bits: the shift base for column 0.
txt_bit() {
} return int (import txt_st)[2];

// How many code points the face has, counting from txt_first().
txt_facen() {
} return int (import txt_st)[3];
